        explain: "The parser tried to load a file cited by an `import \"path\"` directive but `read_file` reported the resolved path does not exist. Common causes: typo in the path string, missing file, wrong directory level (`../` count off by one). Paths are resolved relative to the file containing the import, then normalized (./, ../ collapsed). Stage1 punch list Gap 1 — added when the parser learned to recursively load imports instead of treating them as opaque names.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX0011",
        title: "expected opening brace",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S0",
        template: "expected opening brace to start a block, found {found}",
        explain: "The parser expected an opening brace `{` to begin a block expression (fn body, if/else arm, while body, struct/enum body). Without it, the parser cannot tell where the body starts and may silently absorb the next item into the surrounding context. Stage1 punch list Gap 5 — added so block-open mismatches surface as a precise S0 diagnostic instead of a downstream cascade. Note: braces are escaped from the message template here because `compiler/diag/builder.hexa::_interpolate` treats raw `{...}` as a placeholder boundary.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX0012",
        title: "expected closing brace",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S0",
        template: "expected closing brace to end a block, found {found}",
        explain: "The parser expected a closing brace `}` to end a block expression. Without it, the parser may silently consume the next top-level item into the prior body. Stage1 punch list Gap 5 — added so block-close mismatches surface as a precise S0 diagnostic instead of a downstream cascade. Note: braces are escaped from the message template here because `compiler/diag/builder.hexa::_interpolate` treats raw `{...}` as a placeholder boundary.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX0013",
        title: "array literal too large for the compile path",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S0",
        template: "array literal has {found} elements — exceeds the compile-path cap (1024). Construct at runtime (loop + push) or load via the atlas.n6 SSOT (n6/atlas.n6 + compiler/atlas/merger.hexa). (HXC sidecar path retired 2026-05-22 — PRs #312 + #314.)",
        explain: "GRAMMAR-ENFORCED real-limit (compile path only; the interpreter / self/parser.hexa is exempt and handles arbitrarily large array literals fine — proven O(1) RSS). A source-level array literal with thousands of elements (notably the embedded-atlas const arrays in compiler/atlas/embedded.gen.hexa, ~7400 AtlasNode) makes the new compiler's codegen/error-recovery accumulate super-quadratic (O(n²)) RSS — empirically 231 GiB / 52 s before OOM, the #1 blocker of stage-2/stage-3/E2. Rather than rely on runtime path selection (the prior HXC fast path still left the giant const-literal fallback in the build graph, so it was still codegen'd), the compile path STRUCTURALLY REJECTS the construct so the O(n²) is impossible by grammar, not merely avoided. Fix: build large collections at runtime, or serve them from the atlas.n6 SSOT (governance @D h_atlas_single_export; PR #312 + #314 retired the HXC sidecar 2026-05-22 — static_atlas() now parses n6/atlas.n6 via compiler/atlas/merger.hexa).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX0014",
        title: "defer in unsupported position",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S0",
        template: "`defer` body cannot be used here (found {found}) — use `defer <expr>` or `defer <block>`; assignment bodies and closure bodies are not deferable",
        explain: "HEXA-OWN M1 — `defer` registers a scope-exit action that drains at fn exit in LIFO order (at-most-once per lexical defer, drain-time variable values; gen2 reference semantics, self/parser.hexa parse_defer_stmt + self/codegen.hexa __fn_exit). Two shapes are rejected: (1) an assignment body such as `defer x = 1` — the reference gen2 grammar rejects it, and the native expr body parses via parse_logical_or precisely so an Eq-family token after the body surfaces here instead of being silently absorbed by parse_assignment; (2) `defer` inside a closure body — a closure is a separate call frame and the M1 lowering scopes defer to top-level fn bodies. Wrap the assignment in a block if the intent really is a deferred store; move a closure-body defer into the enclosing fn.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX0015",
        title: "throw requires a value",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S0",
        template: "`throw` requires a value expression starting on the same line, found {found}",
        explain: "Throw-parity with the gen2 reference frontend: gen2's parse_throw_stmt (self/parser.hexa parse_throw_stmt) calls parse_expr UNCONDITIONALLY, so a throw value is required — and gen2's lexer emits Newline tokens (suppressed only inside parens/brackets), so a value that does not start on the keyword's line hits gen2's unknown-token arm ('unexpected token Newline'), the same error class as a bare `throw` before a closing brace, a semicolon, or EOF. The native lexer drops newlines, so the parser mirrors the rule via token span lines and emits this diagnostic WITHOUT consuming the offending token: consuming it (the parse_primary placeholder-Ident fallthrough) would swallow a structural brace/semicolon and desync the enclosing block into a shifted HX0012 cascade. Recovery: the cursor stays on the offending token, which is either an existing resync point or the start of the next sibling statement. A value that STARTS on the keyword's line may still span lines (open call paren / multi-line binop). Note `return` is different by design: a bare `return` is VALID in both frontends (gen2 Newline/Eof/RBrace guard), while a bare `throw` is an error in both.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX1042",
        title: "atlas node not found",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S1",
        template: "atlas {atlas_hash} has no {kind} node `{node_id}`",
        explain: "The identifier was used as a P/C/L/E token, but no such node exists in the atlas snapshot pinned by hexa.toml. This is usually a typo or a missing atlas update.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
