        template: "unhandled HExpr kind `{kind}` in hir_to_mir (default fall-through produced no MIR)",
        explain: "The HIR→MIR lowering pass dispatches on HExpr.kind; unknown kinds previously fell through to a children-recurse-then-return-no-value path that silently produced empty MIR. Stage1 punch list C16 — mirror the Gap 14 lower-diag sink convention: emit HX1103 with the unhandled kind so missing constructs (e.g. growable-array method calls, future for/yield desugarings) surface as a precise S2 diagnostic instead of generating a zero-shaped output.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX1104",
        title: "closure codegen not yet implemented",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S2",
        template: "closure-expression `fn(...){...}` codegen is not yet implemented (RFC closure-expression cycle C1 delivers parse/bind/type only — MIR indirect-call lowering is C2, arm64 codegen is C3)",
        explain: "The closure-expression RFC is split into three cycles. C1 (this build) implements the parser, AST node (ExprKind::Closure), bind-pass scoping of closure params, and a `fn`-typed value for the type checker — the full front-half. Codegen is deliberately deferred: C2 adds the HIR->MIR closure-conversion lowering (capture-environment struct synthesis + closure value + indirect call), and C3 adds the arm64 backend. Until C2/C3 land, a program that uses a closure-expression compiles through parse/check cleanly and then stops here with this accurate diagnostic — instead of the misleading pre-C1 `HX2001 undefined name fn` (which mis-reported the `fn` keyword as an unknown identifier). To proceed today, rewrite the closure as a named top-level `fn` item.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX1100",
        title: "closure capture not yet supported",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S2",
        template: "closure in `{site}` captures variable `{name}` from outer scope; v0 lower only handles non-capturing closures (Stage1 Gap 14.1 follow-up)",
        explain: "Hexa stage1 lowering currently inlines closure bodies at the call site without environment capture. If a closure references a variable defined in an outer scope, the inline path produces an undefined reference at MIR/LIR time. Stage1 Gap 14.1 follow-up will add closure conversion (environment struct synthesis + indirect call). Until then, rewrite the closure to take all dependencies as explicit parameters, or inline the computation. Severity: Warning so a transient capture does not block the build, but the produced binary may have undefined behaviour for the closure path.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX1110",
        title: "host-stdlib import in embedded target",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "host-stdlib import `{path}` is forbidden under --target=`{target}` (embedded targets only allow stdlib/core, stdlib/alloc, stdlib/hal, stdlib/embedded, stdlib/mcu)",
        explain: "Targets matching `*-none-*` (e.g. thumbv7em-none-eabihf, riscv32imac-unknown-none-elf, thumbv6m-none-eabi) are bare-metal: they have no host OS, no libc, no syscall surface. Importing modules from the host stdlib (stdlib/io, stdlib/http, stdlib/json, ...) under these targets pulls in symbols the firmware linker cannot resolve and breaks the no_std contract documented in SPEC.yaml firmware_evolution. Allowed embedded-safe paths: `stdlib/core/*` (pure: int, slice, option, result, math), `stdlib/alloc/*` (allocator interface), `stdlib/hal/*` (hardware abstraction layer), `stdlib/embedded/*` (panic handler, no_std prelude), `stdlib/mcu/*` (MCU-specific drivers). Phase F3 deliverable: target_gate_check enforcement at compile time.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
