        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "duplicate `pub` definition of `{name}`: also defined in `{first_file}` (importer `{second_file}`)",
        explain: "Two distinct files both declare `pub <name>` and end up spliced into the same compilation unit. The pre-pass keeps the first definition encountered and skips the rest, so this is informational unless the two definitions actually disagree — in which case behaviour follows the first definition.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3005",
        title: "match arms have different types",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "match arms have different types: expected `{expected}`, got `{actual}` on arm {pos}",
        explain: "All arms of a `match` used as an expression must produce the same type. Adjust the divergent arm to match the first arm's result type.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3006",
        title: "non-exhaustive match",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "non-exhaustive match on enum `{enum}`: missing variant(s) {missing}; add the arm(s) or a `_` wildcard",
        explain: "A `match` over an enum should cover every variant. This warning implements the single-column case of Maranget's usefulness algorithm (Luc Maranget, \"Warnings for pattern matching\", JFP 2007): the set of head constructors covered by the (unguarded) arms is compared against the enum's full variant signature; any variant not covered AND not subsumed by a `_` wildcard or an irrefutable ident catch-all makes the wildcard row useful, i.e. the match is non-exhaustive. Guarded arms (`Variant if cond`) do NOT count toward coverage because the guard may fail at runtime. This is a non-fatal warning (S3) — codegen still emits a no-value fall-through for an unmatched scrutinee, so existing builds are unaffected. Fix: add the missing variant arms, or a final `_ -> …` wildcard if the omission is intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3011",
        title: "mismatched types",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "mismatched types: expected `{expected}`, found `{actual}`",
        explain: "Static type check (always-on since FLIP-3 flagless — Rust-style, no disable switch). A local binding, assignment, or struct-literal field-init declared (or its place has) an explicit annotation `T` whose primitive type provably differs from the initialiser's inferred type — e.g. `let x: i32 = \"s\"` or `let ok: bool = 1`. This is the Rust E0308 'mismatched types' rule brought to LOCAL (in-fn-body) positions: a string/bool/char literal never coerces to a numeric annotation, and an integer literal never coerces to `bool`. Emitted unconditionally (FLIP-3 flagless) as a build-refusing ERROR for real source (aborts before codegen — REJECT), while byte-eq TEST FIXTURES (`compiler/test/` corpus + `*_test.hexa`, which carry deliberate type-unstable inputs) stay in the non-fatal WARNING band via the `_types_strict_for` path carve-out — so the shipping build never breaks (real source has zero violations; gen3≡gen4 emit byte-identical, byteeq-neutral). There is NO env opt-out or severity switch (Rust-style — type checking is part of the language); the fixture carve-out is the only band decision. Coverage is a scope-local rung set (all KNOWN-scalar-primitive vs KNOWN-scalar-primitive only): r1 scalar-literal RHS on a typed `let`; r2 ident/call RHS on a typed `let`; r3 ident-LHS re-assignment; r4 array-element assign `a[i] = …`; r5 struct-field assign `s.field = …`; r6 struct-literal field-init `Point{ x: … }`; r8b param/module-let array-base index-assign (folded under HEXA_STATIC_TYPES via F0); r9a Index/Field READ inference feeding the let/assign rungs (Index needs ARRAY_LOWER); r9c match literal-pattern vs scrutinee. Array element types flow structurally under the F0-folded array-lower gate, feeding the always-on HX3003/HX3004 sites; struct-literal field EXISTENCE is HX3016 (r9d). Generic/trait static-dispatch and nested receivers (`s.inner.x`) are a measured ceiling — the `_infer_expr` scope cannot reach them (dynamic runtime type-erasure). Fix: change the value to match the annotation, or the annotation to match the value. The integer-literal/float-literal coercions Rust allows (`let x: i32 = 5`, `let y: f64 = 1.5`) still type-check.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3012",
        title: "use of moved value",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "use of moved value `{name}`: this `@own` binding (declared at line {def_line}) was already moved at line {moved_line}",
        explain: "Opt-in ownership lint (`HEXA_OWN_LINT=1`). `@own` marks a `let` binding as a SINGLE-OWNER value: the first time its bare name is consumed by a move site — the RHS of a later `let`, the RHS of an assignment, a call argument, or a bare-ident `return` value — ownership is considered transferred out of the binding. This warning fires on any SECOND read/move of the same binding after that transfer, mirroring Rust's E0382 'borrow of moved value' shape (rustc_borrowck use-after-move) as a non-fatal WARNING: the build still compiles and codegen is untouched — this is a lint band, not a REJECT. Scope is HEXA-OWN L2 r1+r2+r2b: block-local `@own let` bindings inside one fn body, including return position (`return x` warns when `x` was already moved, and itself counts as the move for any later use), and `@own` fn PARAMS — inline `fn f(@own p: [i64])` or the fn-level `@own(\"p\")` spelling — which the body treats affinely (registered at fn entry, moved by the same sites). Copy-class scalar types (integers, floats, bool, char, unit — per the #4088 Copy predicate) are exempt from move tracking entirely. Struct fields, borrows (`&`), and drop insertion are later rungs. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing and populates no registry — the diagnostic stream is byte-identical (byteeq-neutral). Fix: don't reuse the binding after its move — restructure so each owned value has exactly one consumer, clone/recompute the value, or drop the `@own` annotation if the binding is intentionally shared.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3013",
        title: "arena-allocated value escapes to a module global",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "arena escape: fn-local composite `{name}` (declared at line {def_line}) is stored into module global `{global}`; the fn arena is reclaimed on return and only the RETURN value is heapified",
        explain: "Opt-in arena-escape lint (`HEXA_ARENA_ESCAPE_LINT=1`), HEXA-OWN L2.5 r1 Rule B. On the default fn-arena path (hexa_val_arena_on) a composite literal built inside a fn — an array literal `[…]` or struct literal `S{…}` — is bump-allocated in that fn's arena scope. `__hexa_fn_arena_return` heapifies ONLY the fn's RETURN value before `scope_pop` reclaims the arena, and `hexa_array_push` dynamically heapifies push-into-outer-array — but a store into a MODULE-LEVEL `let` global is the runtime-admitted unheapified escape (self/runtime_core.c:3432): after the fn returns, the global references reclaimed arena memory. This lint warns when a fn-local `let` binding whose initialiser was a composite literal is later assigned (bare-ident RHS) to a module-level `let` global not shadowed by a fn-local binding. Non-fatal WARNING band (build still compiles; codegen/runtime untouched — check-side only, native aprime frontend). Scope is L2.5 r1: direct `global = local_composite` assignment inside one fn body; gen2 `@no_arena` contract-violation scanning (Rule A), stores through fields/indices, closure captures, and region-returns depth are later rungs. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing and populates no registry — the diagnostic stream is byte-identical (byteeq-neutral). Fix: return the composite and assign at the caller, build the global's value at module scope, or copy element-by-element into a heap-resident structure instead of aliasing the fn-arena allocation.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3014",
        title: "shared-handle aliasing hazard",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "shared-handle hazard ({rule}): `{name}` is used after `{other}` at line {line}; both names refer to the same underlying value",
        explain: "Opt-in MIR loan pass (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`), HEXA-OWN L3 M3+M4. hexa aggregates (arrays, map-backed structs) are REFERENCE values: `let b = a` copies the handle, not the buffer, so a write through one name is visible through every alias. This warning fires on two shapes, both at HIR→MIR lowering time (native aprime frontend; spans in hand): Rule 1 'aliased write' — a value is used through one name after the SAME underlying value was written through a DIFFERENT name (field-assign `x.f = v`, index-assign `a[i] = v`, or a receiver-mutating method `a.push/pop/truncate/set`) — the mutate-one-alias-then-read-the-other shape that silently observes the mutation; benign read-only aliasing (`let b = a` + reads through both) never fires. Rule 2 'use after move' — a binding whose value was passed into a callee parameter declared `@own` (ownership transfer) is used again; call args into non-`@own` params are READS, never moves, so ordinary calls never fire. Scope (M3) is order-aware within a straight-line block, and (M4, NLL-shaped) FLOW-SENSITIVE across blocks: an end-of-fn event-reachability fixed-point over the MIR CFG (Block.preds/succs) re-checks every use against each write/move whose block reaches the use's block through at least one CFG edge — control-flow joins (write inside a branch, read after the join) and loop back-edges (loop-carried alias: a body block reaches itself through the cycle) are caught; the analysis is path-insensitive (a write on ONE branch arms the join — advisory-band tolerance). Granularity stays whole-local (no place projections in MIR Operands: a write to `x.a` also arms reads of `x.b` — documented ceiling). Aliases created via fn returns, struct-field/array-element loads, cross-param aliasing, and unknown-typed locals are not tracked (precision-first: quiet false-negatives over loud false-positives). Warning band by default under HEXA_BORROWCK; HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1 escalates to a build-refusing error: the pass only observes the lowering (codegen untouched), and under the default warning band the build still compiles, but under STRICT (which implies HEXA_BORROWCK) the SAME precise detection is re-banded to Error in `_emit_hx3014` via the caller severity-override path, so the drain fatal-gate aborts before codegen — detection precision is identical between the two bands (HEXA-OWN L4 Lane-B B0). The default build (both flags unset) populates no registry, runs no fixed-point, and the diagnostic stream plus emitted binary are byte-identical (byteeq-neutral). Fix: don't interleave a write through one alias with uses through another — mutate through a single name, copy the aggregate element-by-element if independent buffers were intended, or restructure so the moved-from binding is not reused.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3016",
        title: "struct has no field with this name",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "struct `{name}` has no field named `{field}`",
        explain: "Static type check (always-on since FLIP-3 flagless — Rust-style, no disable switch), static-types r9d. A struct-literal field-init names a field the struct does not declare — e.g. `Point{ x: 1, z: 2 }` when `Point` declares only `x`/`y`. This is the Rust E0560 'struct has no field named' rule (rustc_hir_typeck check_expr_struct_fields → report_unknown_field), and it also hardens the r6 field-init TYPE check: before r9d a typo'd field name silently skipped both the existence and the type comparison. The check reads the same (struct,field) side registry r5/r6 use; a struct name the registry does not know (cross-module or unresolved receiver) stays SILENT — conservative unknown-fallback, consistent with the ladder's under-reject contract. Missing REQUIRED fields (Rust E0063) are deliberately NOT this diagnostic — that is a separate warning-band follow-on. Emitted unconditionally (FLIP-3 flagless) as a build-refusing ERROR for real source (REJECT), while byte-eq TEST FIXTURES (`compiler/test/` corpus + `*_test.hexa`) stay WARNING via the `_types_strict_for` path carve-out — so the shipping build never breaks (real source has zero violations, byteeq-neutral). No env opt-out or severity switch (Rust-style); the fixture carve-out is the only band decision. Fix: correct the field name, or add the field to the struct declaration.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3017",
        title: "struct literal is missing a required field",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "struct `{name}` is missing field `{field}`",
        explain: "Static type check (always-on since FLIP-3 flagless — Rust-style, no disable switch), static-types r11. A struct-literal OMITS a field the struct DECLARES — e.g. `Point{ x: 1 }` when `Point` declares both `x` AND `y`. This is the Rust E0063 'missing field in initializer' rule (rustc_hir_typeck check_expr_struct_fields → report_missing_fields), the PAIR of HX3016 (E0560, a PRESENT field the struct does not declare): HX3016 fires when a provided field is unknown, HX3017 when a declared field is absent. The check reads the same (struct,field) side registry r5/r6/r9d use; it computes (declared_fields − provided_fields) and emits one HX3017 per missing declared field. A struct name the registry does not know (cross-module or unresolved receiver) stays SILENT — the same conservative unknown-fallback as HX3016. The SAME band policy as HX3011/HX3016 under FLIP-3 flagless: a build-refusing ERROR unconditionally for real source (REJECT), while byte-eq TEST FIXTURES (`compiler/test/` corpus + `*_test.hexa`) stay WARNING via the `_types_strict_for` path carve-out — so the shipping build never breaks (real source has zero violations, byteeq-neutral). No env opt-out or severity switch (Rust-style); the fixture carve-out is the only band decision. Fix: add the missing field(s) to the literal, or give the struct a default so the omission is intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3018",
        title: "non-exhaustive match (fatal)",
        // Error, not Warning: this is the SAME usefulness analysis as
        // HX3006, escalated to a build-breaking error under the opt-in
        // `HEXA_EXHAUSTIVE_FATAL=1` gate (default-OFF). With both gates
        // unset the default build emits neither HX3006 nor HX3018, so the
        // diagnostic stream AND emitted bytes are byte-identical to before
        // — release integrity first. This mirrors Rust, where a non-
        // exhaustive match is a hard error (E0004 "non-exhaustive
        // patterns"), not a lint: rustc's `report_non_exhaustive_match`
        // raises it via `struct_span_code_err!(..., E0004, ...)` in
        // compiler/rustc_mir_build/src/thir/pattern/check_match.rs, so a
        // program with an uncovered variant fails to compile. Selecting
        // HEXA_EXHAUSTIVE_FATAL=1 opts a strict/CI build into that same
        // Rust-grade guarantee while keeping the default build untouched.
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "non-exhaustive match on enum `{enum}`: missing variant(s) {missing}; add the arm(s) or a `_` wildcard",
        explain: "A `match` over an enum must cover every variant. This is the fatal form of the non-exhaustiveness diagnostic (see HX3006 for the underlying Maranget 2007 usefulness analysis): under `HEXA_EXHAUSTIVE_FATAL=1` an uncovered variant aborts the build before codegen instead of merely warning, matching Rust's E0004 hard-error semantics. The default build (both HEXA_EXHAUSTIVE and HEXA_EXHAUSTIVE_FATAL unset) emits neither HX3006 nor HX3018, so opting in changes nothing for unaffected builds. Fix: add the missing variant arms, or a final `_ -> …` wildcard if the omission is intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3019",
        title: "returning a borrow of a function-local value",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the PAIR of the HX3014 loan-pass
        // family: the opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`) surfaces a
        // returned reference to a value owned by the current function. Under
        // `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance is re-banded to
        // a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller override in
        // `_bck_emit_ret_escape` (identical mechanism to HX3014). With the flag
        // unset the loan pass is inert (`_bck_active` false), so neither this
        // nor HX3014 is emitted and the diagnostic stream + emitted bytes are
        // byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "`{name}` returns a borrow of function-local `{origin}` (borrow created at line {borrow_line}) — the value is dropped at the end of the function",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN L5-E1) found a `return` whose value is a reference into a value OWNED by the current function — either directly (`return &x` / `return &mut x` over a non-global local or by-value parameter) or one hop through a ref binding (`let r = &x; …; return r`). This is Rust's E0515 ('cannot return reference to local variable / temporary value'; rustc_borrowck reports it when the returned region outlives the borrowed local's storage): the borrowed value's storage is reclaimed when the function returns, so the returned reference would dangle. The detection is conservative — a borrow of a module-level global is NOT flagged (its storage outlives the call), and a ref binding that is re-let or reassigned to a non-borrow RHS before the return is treated as detached (the loan is killed, newest-entry-per-name wins). Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: return the value by move/copy instead of by borrow, or restructure so the borrowed value outlives the caller (e.g. accept an `&mut` out-param, or return an owned clone).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3021",
        title: "conflicting borrow while a mutable borrow is live",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the PAIR of the HX3019 loan-pass
        // family: the opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`) surfaces a
        // second concurrent borrow of a value that already has a live mutable
        // loan (or a new mutable borrow while any loan is live). Under
        // `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance is re-banded to
        // a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller override in
        // `_bck_emit_borrow_conflict` (identical mechanism to HX3014/HX3019).
        // With the flag unset the loan pass is inert (`_bck_active` false), so
        // nothing is emitted and the diagnostic stream + emitted bytes are
        // byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot borrow `{origin}` as {new_kind} for `{name}` because it is also borrowed as {other_kind} by `{other}` at line {other_line}",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN E2+E3) found a second live borrow of a value that is already borrowed incompatibly. hexa uses the same aliasing-XOR-mutation model as Rust: a value may have EITHER any number of shared (`&`) borrows OR exactly one mutable (`&mut`) borrow live at once, never both. This is Rust's E0502 ('cannot borrow X as _ because it is also borrowed as mutable'; rustc_borrowck reports it when two conflicting loans overlap in scope). The E2 loan registry records each `let r = &x` / `let r = &mut x` over a non-global function-local as a loan (name → origin, mutability bit, live via kill-on-reassign); at a NEW borrow of the same origin E3 scans for a conflicting LIVE loan and fires when the existing loan is `&mut` (any second borrow is illegal) OR the new borrow is `&mut` (a mutable borrow demands exclusivity). Two shared borrows (`&` while `&`) never conflict. The detection is conservative / flow-insensitive within a scope: only same-fn live loans count, a loan reassigned to a non-borrow RHS is killed (newest-entry-per-name wins), and borrows created through a call argument, a struct field / array element, or a closure are NOT tracked (precision-first — quiet false-negatives over loud false-positives). Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: end the first borrow before starting the second (borrows end at last use under NLL), or take a single borrow of the right mutability and thread it through instead of aliasing.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3023",
        title: "cannot assign to a borrowed value",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the write-through rung of the E2
        // loan-registry family: the opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`)
        // surfaces an assignment to a value that already has a LIVE loan (a
        // write through the original storage invalidates the borrow). Under
        // `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance is re-banded to
        // a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller override in
        // `_bck_emit_write_through` (identical mechanism to HX3014/HX3019/
        // HX3021). With the flag unset the loan pass is inert (`_bck_active`
        // false), so nothing is emitted and the diagnostic stream + emitted
        // bytes are byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot assign to `{origin}` because it is borrowed by `{name}` at line {borrow_line}",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN E4) found an assignment `x = …` (or any write-through to the original storage) while a LIVE loan of `x` exists in the E2 registry — i.e. some binding `r` was borrowed against `x` (`let r = &x` / `let r = &mut x`) earlier in the same function and has not been killed (by reassigning `r` to a non-borrow RHS) before this write. This is Rust's E0506 ('cannot assign to `x` because it is borrowed'; rustc_borrowck reports it when a write to a place overlaps a live loan of that place): the write would invalidate the borrow's aliasing guarantee — a `&mut x` loan promises exclusive write access through `r`, and even a shared `&x` loan promises the observed value is stable, so overwriting `x` directly breaks both. The E4 scan runs in the assign-arm of `_lower_hexpr` IMMEDIATELY before the L5-E1 ref-kill block, so it observes the registry state BEFORE the assigned name's own loan (if any) is detached — the scan keys on `origin == lhs.text`, so it matches loans OF the assigned name regardless of mutability (a write invalidates both `&` and `&mut` loans). The detection is conservative / flow-insensitive within a scope (no NLL last-use yet): a loan stays LIVE from its `let` to the end of the function or until the binding is reassigned to a non-borrow, so `let r = &x; <use r>; x = 5;` ALSO fires even though Rust NLL (last-use) would end `r`'s loan before the assign — this is the documented flow-insensitive false-positive class, the SAME conservative-loud-over-quiet policy as E2/E3 (precision-first: a real hazard is never missed, a benign cascade is warned rather than silently accepted). Only same-fn live loans count; borrows of module-level globals, through call args / struct fields / array elements / closures, and cross-function loans are NOT tracked (documented false-negatives). Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: end the borrow before writing (reassign the borrowed binding to a non-borrow RHS, or restructure so the write precedes the borrow), or write through the borrow itself (`*r = …`) once place-projection loans are tracked.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3027",
        title: "cannot use a value while it is mutably borrowed",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the use-of-value rung of the E2
        // loan-registry family: the opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`)
        // surfaces a READ of a value while it has a LIVE `&mut` loan. Under
        // `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance is re-banded to
        // a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller override in
        // `_bck_emit_use_while_mut` (identical mechanism to HX3014/HX3019/
        // HX3021/HX3023). With the flag unset the loan pass is inert
        // (`_bck_active` false), so nothing is emitted and the diagnostic stream
        // + emitted bytes are byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot use `{origin}` because it is mutably borrowed by `{name}` at line {borrow_line}",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN L5-E5) found a USE (a read of the value) of `x` while a LIVE MUTABLE loan of `x` exists in the E2 registry — i.e. some binding `m` was mutably borrowed against `x` (`let m = &mut x`) earlier in the same function and has not been killed (by reassigning `m` to a non-borrow RHS) before this read. This is Rust's E0503 ('cannot use `x` because it was mutably borrowed'; rustc_borrowck reports it when a use of a place overlaps a live mutable loan of that place): a `&mut x` loan promises EXCLUSIVE access through `m`, so reading `x` directly through its own name while that promise is outstanding would observe a value the mutable borrower may be about to change. UNLIKE the write-through rule (E0506/HX3023, which invalidates BOTH `&` and `&mut` loans because any write breaks aliasing), E0503 keys on the MUTABILITY bit: a shared `&x` loan permits concurrent reads, so a use of `x` while only shared loans are live is SILENT — the rule fires only when `_bck_ref_mut` is set. The scan runs at the single ident READ funnel (`_bck_check_use`, HEXA-OWN L3 M3), before the aggregate-group early-return, so a scalar origin (`let mut x = 5`) is covered even though it is never group-tracked. A borrow RHS is EXEMPT: `let n = &mut x` reads `x` to take its ADDRESS (not to use its value), so a re-borrow of an already-`&mut`-loaned origin is reported by the borrow-conflict rule (E0502/HX3021) alone, never double-reported here (the `_bck_in_borrow_rhs` suppression). The detection is conservative / flow-insensitive within a scope (no NLL last-use yet): a loan stays LIVE from its `let` to the end of the function or until the binding is reassigned to a non-borrow, so `let m = &mut x; <m unused>; let y = x;` ALSO fires even though Rust NLL (last-use) would end `m`'s loan before the read — this is the documented flow-insensitive false-positive class, the SAME conservative-loud-over-quiet policy as E2/E3/E4. Only same-fn live loans count; borrows of module-level globals, through call args / struct fields / array elements / closures, and cross-function loans are NOT tracked (documented false-negatives). Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: finish the mutable borrow before reading the value (end `m`'s last use, or reassign `m` to a non-borrow RHS before the read), or read through the borrow itself (`*m`) instead of the original name.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3024",
        title: "cannot apply unary operator `-`",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot apply unary operator `-` to type `{actual}`",
        explain: "Static type check (always-on since FLIP-3 flagless — Rust-style, no disable switch), static-types segment(b). A unary negation `-x` whose operand's inferred static type is a KNOWN non-numeric scalar (`string` or `char`) can never succeed: codegen lowers `-x` as `hexa_sub(0, x)` on every target, and both runtime dispatchers (stdlib rt_sub and the C-substrate hexa_sub) throw 'cannot subtract non-numeric operand' at run time. This is the Rust E0600 'cannot apply unary operator' rule (rustc_hir_typeck op.rs lookup_op_method failure → E0600), the unary mirror of the always-on binop arithmetic mismatch (HX3001). Conservative under-reject: it fires ONLY when the operand type is the KNOWN scalar string/char — unknown idents (empty kind), `unit` (unresolved-callee sentinel), HexaVal/any dynamics, aggregates, and generics all stay SILENT. `bool` is deliberately EXEMPT: the runtime legalizes `-true` via bool→int coercion (_HX_COERCE_BOOL / the rt_sub bool arm), and the checker matches runtime policy exactly (the same reason string `+` concatenation is legal at the HX3001 binop site). Logical `!x` is NEVER flagged — it lowers through hexa_truthy, which accepts every tag; `~x` is likewise out of scope. Emitted unconditionally (FLIP-3 flagless) as a build-refusing ERROR for real source (REJECT), while byte-eq TEST FIXTURES (`compiler/test/` corpus + `*_test.hexa`) stay WARNING via the `_types_strict_for` path carve-out. No env opt-out or severity switch. Fix: convert the operand to a number first (e.g. parse the string), or fix the producing expression.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3025",
        title: "cannot index into a non-indexable type",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot index into a value of type `{actual}`",
        explain: "Static type check (always-on since FLIP-3 flagless — Rust-style, no disable switch), static-types segment(b). A subscript `x[i]` whose base's inferred static type is a KNOWN non-container scalar (`int`/`float`/`bool`/`char`) can never succeed: the C-substrate `hexa_array_get` wrapper aborts at run time on a non-array tag ('container is not an array'), so the index is a guaranteed runtime error surfaced to S3 (the same 'guaranteed-runtime-error → REJECT' contract as HX3024). This is the Rust E0608 'cannot index into a value of type `{actual}`' rule (rustc_hir_typeck failing to find an `Index` impl for a scalar). Conservative under-reject: it fires for the KNOWN scalars int/float/bool/char AND for a KNOWN `string` base — an `s[i]` GET also aborts at runtime (the C-substrate wrapper detects the string tag and throws `string[i]: use .chars()[i] or substring`), so the same 'guaranteed-runtime-error → REJECT' contract covers it; the fix is `.chars()[i]` or `substring`. Unknown idents (empty kind), `unit`, HexaVal/any dynamics, `array`, `map`, and `named` (struct/enum, which may define indexing) all stay SILENT. Emitted unconditionally (FLIP-3 flagless) as a build-refusing ERROR for real source (REJECT), while byte-eq TEST FIXTURES (`compiler/test/` corpus + `*_test.hexa`) stay WARNING via the `_types_strict_for` path carve-out. No env opt-out or severity switch. Fix: index an array/collection instead, or fix the producing expression so the base is a container.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3026",
        title: "no field on a primitive type",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "`{actual}` is a primitive type and therefore has no field `{field}`",
        explain: "Static type check (always-on since FLIP-3 flagless — Rust-style, no disable switch), static-types segment(b). A field READ `x.f` whose receiver's inferred static type is a KNOWN non-aggregate scalar (`int`/`float`/`bool`/`char`) can never succeed: a scalar HexaVal carries no field slots, so the runtime field access is meaningless / a guaranteed error (the same 'known-runtime-error → REJECT' contract as HX3024/HX3025). This is the Rust E0610 rule (rustc_hir_typeck: '`{actual}` is a primitive type and therefore doesn't have fields'). It fires ONLY at the r9a field-READ site (`_infer_expr` Field arm), NOT in callee position: `n.foo()` infers the SAME `Field{foo}` node as the call's callee, where the unresolved-callee HX2001 already fires — so the checker raises an internal callee-depth guard around that infer (`_types_callee_depth`) and this arm stays SILENT there (one mistake = one diagnostic). Builtin methods (`n.len`, `n.count`, … — the ~90-member `_is_builtin_method` set) are codegen receiver-dispatch, not fields, so they are EXEMPT (returning a field type for a legal builtin-method selector would false-fire elsewhere). Conservative under-reject: it fires ONLY for the KNOWN scalars int/float/bool/char — unknown idents (empty kind), `unit`, HexaVal/any dynamics, `array`, `map`, and `named` (struct/enum, which DO have fields) all stay SILENT; a nested receiver (`s.inner.x`) whose type erases to unknown is also silent (the documented r7+ type-erasure ceiling). Emitted unconditionally (FLIP-3 flagless) as a build-refusing ERROR for real source (REJECT), while byte-eq TEST FIXTURES (`compiler/test/` corpus + `*_test.hexa`) stay WARNING via the `_types_strict_for` path carve-out. No env opt-out or severity switch. Fix: access a field of a struct/aggregate instead, or fix the producing expression so the receiver is an aggregate.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3028",
        title: "cannot borrow a value as mutable more than once at a time",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the E0499 faithfulness split of the
        // E2 loan-registry borrow-conflict family: the opt-in borrow checker
        // (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`) surfaces a SECOND `&mut` borrow of a value whose
        // first `&mut` loan is still live — the double-mutable-borrow case rustc
        // reports with a DISTINCT code (E0499) from the shared-vs-mutable E0502
        // (HX3021). Under `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance
        // is re-banded to a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller
        // override in `_bck_emit_double_mut` (identical mechanism to HX3014/
        // HX3019/HX3021/HX3023/HX3027). With the flag unset the loan pass is
        // inert (`_bck_active` false), so nothing is emitted and the diagnostic
        // stream + emitted bytes are byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot borrow `{origin}` as mutable more than once at a time (for `{name}`) — first mutable borrow by `{other}` at line {other_line} is still live",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN E3.9) found a SECOND mutable borrow of a value whose first mutable borrow is still live: `let a = &mut x; let b = &mut x;`. hexa uses the same aliasing-XOR-mutation model as Rust — a value may have EITHER any number of shared (`&`) borrows OR exactly one mutable (`&mut`) borrow live at once. This is the SPECIFIC mut-while-mut case, which rustc reports with its OWN diagnostic code E0499 ('cannot borrow `x` as mutable more than once at a time'; rustc_borrowck report_conflicting_borrow splits the two-mutable case out from the shared-vs-mutable E0502): a `&mut` loan promises EXCLUSIVE access, so a concurrent second `&mut` would alias two exclusive references. It is the faithfulness split of HX3021/E0502 — the shared conflict cases (a new `&` while a `&mut` is live, or a new `&mut` while a `&` is live) stay on HX3021; ONLY the both-mutable case is re-coded here. The split fires at the SINGLE E3 conflict branch (`_bck_ref_find_conflict` >= 0 with `_bck_ref_mut[cfi] && new_mut`), so HX3028 and HX3021 share one same-site dedup array with zero contention. The detection is conservative / flow-insensitive within a scope (no NLL last-use yet): only same-fn live loans count, a loan reassigned to a non-borrow RHS is killed (newest-entry-per-name wins), and borrows created through a call argument, a struct field / array element, or a closure are NOT tracked — this is the documented flow-insensitive false-positive class (a re-borrow that Rust NLL would allow after the first loan's last use still fires here), the SAME conservative-loud-over-quiet policy as E2/E3/E4/E5. Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: end the first mutable borrow before starting the second (borrows end at last use under NLL), or take a single `&mut` and thread it through instead of aliasing.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3029",
        title: "cannot move out of a value while it is borrowed",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the move-out rung of the E2
        // loan-registry family: the opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`)
        // surfaces a MOVE of a value (out via an `@own` call arg) while it has a
        // LIVE loan. Under `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance
        // is re-banded to a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller
        // override in `_bck_emit_move_while_borrowed` (identical mechanism to
        // HX3014/HX3019/HX3021/HX3023/HX3027). With the flag unset the loan pass
        // is inert (`_bck_active` false), so nothing is emitted and the diagnostic
        // stream + emitted bytes are byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot move out of `{origin}` because it is borrowed by `{name}` at line {borrow_line}",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN L5-E6) found a MOVE of `x` — a bare-ident argument passed to a resolved callee whose corresponding parameter is declared `@own`, which moves the value out of `x`'s binding (the M3 move classification, `_bck_note_move`) — while a LIVE loan of `x` exists in the E2 registry: some binding `r` was borrowed against `x` (`let r = &x` / `let r = &mut x`) earlier in the same function and has not been killed (by reassigning `r` to a non-borrow RHS) before this move. This is Rust's E0505 ('cannot move out of `x` because it is borrowed'; rustc_borrowck reports it when a move of a place overlaps a live loan of that place): moving the value out invalidates the borrow's aliasing guarantee — a `&mut x` loan promises exclusive access through `r`, and even a shared `&x` loan promises the borrowed storage stays put, so moving `x` away breaks BOTH. The move-out scan (`_bck_ref_find_write_through`) is therefore MUTABILITY-BLIND (unlike E0503/HX3027 use-while-mut, which keys on the mutability bit) — the SAME symmetry break as the write-through rule (E0506/HX3023). It runs at the @own call-arg move site (`_lower_hexpr` call arm, HEXA-OWN L3 M3), immediately after the move is recorded, and reuses the E4 write-through scan. Dedup contract (shared-loan primary): when the live loan is `&mut`, the arg ident's own READ (lowered a line earlier) already fired HX3027 (E0503) at THIS span, so the shared same-site dedup array (`_bck_e_lines`/`_bck_e_cols`) collapses HX3029 into it — a `&x` (shared) loan reports HX3029, a `&mut x` loan reports HX3027 alone, never both at one move site. The detection is conservative / flow-insensitive within a scope (no NLL last-use yet): a loan stays LIVE from its `let` to the end of the function or until the binding is reassigned to a non-borrow, so `let r = &x; g(x); <r unused>` ALSO fires even though Rust NLL would end `r`'s loan before the move — the documented flow-insensitive false-positive class, the SAME conservative-loud-over-quiet policy as E2/E3/E4/E5. Only same-fn live loans count; borrows of module-level globals, through call args / struct fields / array elements / closures, and cross-function loans are NOT tracked (documented false-negatives). Move classification itself fires ONLY at `@own` call args (the polarity inversion vs every-arg-moves), and `@own` inline-carrier adoption is currently 0 in the corpus, so even with the flag ON this rule fires on ZERO real source (corpus-clean). Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: end the borrow before the move (reassign the borrowing binding to a non-borrow RHS, or restructure so the move precedes the borrow), or pass a borrow / clone instead of moving the owned value.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3031",
        title: "cannot assign to a value behind a `&` reference",
        // Warning band (SSOT F4 warn-first), the assign-behind-shared-ref rung
        // of the E2 loan-registry family: the opt-in borrow checker
        // (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`) surfaces a WRITE through a binding that is itself a
        // LIVE SHARED (`&`) borrow — `let r = &s; r.x = 1` / `r[i] = v`. A `&`
        // borrow grants read-only access; only `&mut` permits write-through.
        // Under `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` the SAME diagnostic instance is re-banded
        // to a build-refusing Error via the sanctioned caller override in
        // `_bck_emit_assign_behind_ref` (identical mechanism to HX3023/3027/3029).
        // With the flag unset the loan pass is inert (`_bck_active` false), so
        // nothing is emitted and the diagnostic stream + emitted bytes are
        // byte-identical — release integrity first.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "cannot assign through `{name}` because it is behind a `&` reference — `{name}` is a shared borrow of `{origin}` (line {borrow_line})",
        explain: "The opt-in borrow checker (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`, HEXA-OWN L5-E7) found an ASSIGNMENT through a binding that is itself a LIVE SHARED (`&`) reference: `let r = &s; r.x = 1` or `let r = &arr; r[i] = v`. A shared `&` borrow grants READ-only access to the borrowed value; writing through it would mutate storage that other shared borrows assume is stable — only a `&mut` borrow permits write-through. This is Rust's E0594 ('cannot assign to `*r`, which is behind a `&` reference'; rustc_borrowck reports it when an assignment place resolves through a shared reference). The check fires at the field-set and index-set lowering hooks (`_lower_hexpr` assign arms, HEXA-OWN L3): after recording the write (`_bck_note_write` for Rule 1), it looks up the LHS base ident in the E2 loan registry (`_bck_find_ref`) and, when the newest live row for that name is a NON-mutable loan (`origin != \"\"` AND `_bck_ref_mut` false), emits HX3031 against the shared loan. Fires ONLY for `&` loans — writing through `&mut` is the loan's whole purpose (no arg for borrow kind). Conservative / flow-insensitive within a scope (no NLL last-use yet): a loan stays LIVE from its `let` to end-of-function or until the binding is reassigned to a non-borrow RHS (KILL row → `origin == \"\"` → silent, so `let r = &s; r = other; r.x = 1` does not fire); only same-fn loans count; borrows through call args / struct fields / array elements / closures and cross-function loans are NOT tracked (documented false-negatives). A bare ident reassignment `r = v` is NOT flagged (that is a rebind, not a deref-write — hexa has no `*r`), and nested places `r.a.b` are already excluded by the existing single-ident base gate. No new borrow-checker state is introduced — the E2 loan registry is reused as-is. `&`/`&mut` inline-carrier adoption is currently ~0 in the corpus, so even with the flag ON this rule fires on ZERO real source (corpus-clean). Warning band by default (SSOT F4 warn-first); `HEXA_BORROWCK_STRICT=1` re-bands it to a build-refusing Error. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing here and is byte-identical. Fix: take a `&mut` borrow instead of `&` if you need to write through it, or mutate the owner directly rather than through the shared reference.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX4001",
        title: "integer division domain narrow",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S4",
        template: "integer division narrows the real domain to integers; use `//` (floor) or cast to f64 to silence",
        explain: "Dividing two integer operands with `/` silently truncates the real-number result. RFC-017 S4 surfaces this as a domain hint so the author confirms the narrowing was intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX5001",
        title: "unit mismatch in arithmetic",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S5",
        template: "unit mismatch in `{op}`: lhs is `{lhs}`, rhs is `{rhs}`",
        explain: "Addition, subtraction, and comparison operators require both sides to share the same dimensional unit (RFC-017 §3.1 S5). Multiplication and division compose units; only +/-/comparisons require equality. Lenient v1: an untyped operand is compatible with any unit.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX5002",
        title: "fn return unit mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S5",
        template: "return unit mismatch: function `{name}` declares `{expected}`, body produces `{actual}`",
        explain: "A `return` expression (or a fn's trailing block value) yields a value whose dimensional unit does not match the unit declared in the fn's `@units` annotation.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX5003",
        title: "call argument unit mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S5",
        template: "argument {pos} of `{name}`: expected unit `{expected}`, got `{actual}`",
        explain: "A call argument's dimensional unit does not match the corresponding parameter unit declared on the callee's `@units` annotation. Convert the argument or fix the callee declaration.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX6001",
        title: "equational normalization mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S6",
        template: "@verify equational mismatch in `{name}`: LHS normalizes to `{lhs}`, RHS normalizes to `{rhs}`",
        explain: "The `@verify` annotation requested an equational check, but after constant folding, commutative operand sorting, and associativity flattening the LHS and RHS of the body's `==` comparison reduce to different canonical forms. RFC-017 §3.1 S6 — the in-house prover's first-line equational rewrite engine cannot prove the stated equality syntactically. Fix the body to express a true identity, or fall back to `@prove` for SMT-class reasoning.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX6002",
        title: "sample-eval counter-example found",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S6",
        template: "@verify counter-example in `{name}`: at {assignment} LHS evaluates to `{lhs}` but RHS evaluates to `{rhs}`",
        explain: "The sample-eval prover stage exercised the body's `==` comparison at 16 deterministic integer assignments (LCG seed=1, range [-10, 10]) and found at least one input where LHS and RHS disagree. The reported assignment is a concrete witness — the equality cannot hold over Int. Refine the body, narrow domain via `@units`, or upgrade to `@prove` for SMT-grade verification.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8001",
        title: "atlas L node missing for @implements",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "atlas {atlas_hash} has no L node `{node_id}` cited by @implements — fix the citation id, or if L[{node_id}] should exist, register it: `@discover(kind=\"L\")` (upstream: `hexa atlas register <file>` → `promote` if you own hexa-lang **||** `pr` otherwise).",
        explain: "An `@implements(L[id])` annotation refers to an L node that is not present in the merged atlas snapshot. Either fix the citation id (typo / wrong node), or — if the law genuinely belongs in the atlas — register it with `@discover(kind=\"L\")` and land it via the `hexa atlas` CLI: `hexa atlas register <file.hexa>` writes the proposed shard; `hexa atlas promote --staging <shard>` folds it into the live atlas (the `update` arm — repo owners; emits atlas.append.<date>.n6); or `hexa atlas pr` branches + `gh pr create`s against origin (the `PR` arm — non-owners).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8002",
        title: "@implements points at non-L node kind",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "@implements({kind}[{node_id}]) points at a {kind} node; only L nodes may be implemented",
        explain: "The `@implements` annotation is reserved for citing an L (law) node from the atlas. Citing a P/C/E node is a kind error — use the appropriate annotation or cite the law that governs the node.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8003",
        title: "fn uses atlas L refs but has no @implements citation",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "function `{name}` references atlas L node `{node_id}` in its body but has no @implements citation",
        explain: "A function body cites one or more L nodes (via L[id] expressions) but the function declares no `@implements` annotation. Adding `@implements(L[id])` records the law-of-record so downstream verification (S6/S7 prover) can attach proofs to the right node. DEPRECATED: superseded by HX8004 (Error) per SPEC.yaml atlas_citation_strict; kept transitionally for tooling parity. Will be retired in the next citation pass refresh.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8004",
        title: "formula has no atlas binding",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "formula in `{name}` has no atlas binding — `@implements(L[id])` to cite an existing law, or `@discover(kind=\"L\")` to register a new one (upstream: `hexa atlas register <file>` → `promote` if you own hexa-lang **||** `pr` otherwise). Bypass: `@grace(HX8004, until=\"...\", reason=\"...\")` — never silent (emits HX9000). SPEC.yaml atlas_citation_strict.",
        explain: "Functions carrying @verify or @law annotations, OR whose body references atlas L[*] nodes, are knowledge-bearing artifacts — they must be bound to atlas L. Cite an existing law with `@implements(L[id])`, or register a new one with `@discover(kind=\"L\")`. Land it upstream via the `hexa atlas` CLI: `hexa atlas register <file.hexa>` writes the proposed shard (compiler/discover/staging.hexa); then `hexa atlas promote --staging <shard>` folds it into the live atlas if you own hexa-lang (the `update` arm — emits atlas.append.<date>.n6, so the atlas grows with every contribution); or `hexa atlas pr` branches + `gh pr create`s against origin (the `PR` arm — non-owners). Bypass while a migration is in flight: `@grace(HX8004, until, reason)` — never silently (every @grace site emits HX9000 and the build is gated on acked-grace consent).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX9000",
        title: "@grace requires reviewer acknowledgement",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "runtime",
        template: "ai-native: @grace at {file}:{line} suppresses {error_code} until {until}. Reason: \"{reason}\". Suppressed [{suppressed_severity_original}]: {suppressed_title} — {suppressed_message}. This is a TEMPORARY debt — the author is responsible for resolving the underlying issue before the expiry date. Reviewer acknowledgement REQUIRED before merge.",
        explain: "Every @grace site emits this warning to prevent silent strict-lint bypass. The build is gated on explicit user consent at the CI/PR layer: PR description must contain `Acked-grace: HXxxxx by <reviewer>` trailer or commit message must contain `acked-grace: <code> by <author>`. The `Suppressed [...]` segment inlines the original diagnostic's severity / title / short-rendered body (SPEC.yaml opt_out.ai_native_warn_policy.suppressed_diagnostic_inlining, args_keys: suppressed_title / suppressed_message / suppressed_severity_original) so the bypassed text stays auditable at every compile. SPEC.yaml opt_out.ai_native_warn_policy.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX9001",
        title: "@grace expired",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "runtime",
        template: "@grace for {error_code} expired on {until}; fix or extend",
        explain: "An @grace annotation past its `until` date no longer suppresses the underlying diagnostic. Either fix the issue or update `until` (and `reason`).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8010",
        title: "@law annotation malformed",
